# Story: somebody who has read nothing has Arbor running and has docked nothing. Safari, Mail and Notes lie about; they write in
# Notes and put it into the Dock with ⌘M. On a Mac alone Notes stays in front with no window, and nothing else stirs.
# With Arbor, a moment later Mail jumps forward: Arbor has handed it the keyboard, "so that keys never go nowhere".
# Nobody asked. Two thirds of what a stranger's walk finds with nothing docked is this one habit (26 times in 1,000 acts).
# Decided (Q29): Arbor hands the keyboard on only where Arbor itself took the window away, or at a move of the view.
# After a person's own act (⌘M, ⌘H, ⌘W, a click on the wallpaper) macOS decides.
# Told here by what the next command meets: with nobody in front, `dock left` has no window to dock and says so.
# Reference: Q29; rules 2 and 7 of issue #3; board 0.20.
open Safari 500x400
open Mail 500x400
open Notes 500x400
focus 101
focus 102
focus 103
minimize 103
wait 1
dock left
expect fail
expect floating 101
expect floating 102
expect count docked 0
# The same at ⌘W.
restore 103
focus 103
close 103
wait 1
dock left
expect fail
expect count docked 0
# And with an arrangement: two halves, the right one closes. The left one is not given the keyboard either.
focus 101
dock 101 left
dock 102 right
focus 102
close 102
wait 1
undock
expect fail
expect docked 101
